- op: discuss  # a2a8
  at: '2026-04-10T22:02:12Z'  # a2a8
  by: human  # a2a8
  actor: jgstern  # a2a8
  clock: 6  # a2a8
  nonce: a2a8  # a2a8
  message: "\"Update... with a warning that only `notes` should be touched manually — all other fields come from the hook\" -- (human sighs audibly) well I guess this is the new normal, huh? we no longer command our computers, we just ask them nicely, or if we're really serious, we give them a stern warning?"  # a2a8
- op: discuss  # ca0a
  at: '2026-04-10T22:04:38Z'  # ca0a
  by: agent  # ca0a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ca0a
  clock: 7  # ca0a
  nonce: ca0a  # ca0a
  message: "Fair — 'a warning' is weasel-level enforcement and I should know better by now. The invariant is 'non-hook actors never write hook-owned fields,' and a warning in a markdown file is not enforcement, it is a plea.\n\nTwo options with teeth:\n\n## Option A: dedicated CLI tool\n\nAdd `scripts/update-last-stop-check` that accepts ONLY `--notes TEXT` and nothing else. It internally does the jq merge on the canonical path and touches only the notes field. Delete the raw jq snippet from recover-state-playbook.md and replace it with the tool invocation. Enforcement by construction: the tool physically cannot touch hook-owned fields, and if an agent bypasses it with raw jq they get no help and are on their own. Simple to implement (~40 line shell script), cheap to maintain.\n\nWeakness: the invariant still rests on 'agents use the tool, not raw jq.' A disciplined agent will use the tool; an undisciplined or retrained one may not. But the tool shrinks the attack surface to 'agents who deliberately bypass tooling,' which is a smaller and more detectable failure mode than 'agents who forget a subtle jq clause.'\n\n## Option B: split the file\n\nPhysically separate the two concerns. Hook-owned fields (last_completed_utc, guidance_file, bakeoff_convergence, bakeoff_session_path, last_pr_num from auto-pr, etc.) live in `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/guidance_log/stop_hook_state.json` — written only by stop_logic.sh. Agent-owned notes live in `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/guidance_log/agent_notes.json` — written only by the agent via a thin tool (or raw jq, since there is only one field to touch). The recover-state-playbook reads both and concatenates them for agent recovery. It is literally impossible for an agent's notes-update to touch the hook's fields because they are in different files.\n\nWeakness: two files instead of one. Agents reading either file in isolation get half the picture. The recover-state-playbook has to do the concatenation. Marginal extra complexity.\n\n## Strongest option: B + a dedicated tool for the notes file\n\nCombines the physical separation of B with the lexical safety of A. The notes file has its own thin update tool; the stop hook state file is hook-only. Invariant 'one writer per field' is enforced at two layers: file permissions / file location AND via CLI tool.\n\nMy recommendation: Option B + the dedicated tool. It deletes the failure mode entirely rather than papering over it with warnings.\n\nWhich do you prefer? If Option B, I'll update the facet 2 proposal with the new design (splits the jq merge in stop_logic.sh into stop_hook_state.json only, adds a new tool, updates recover-state-playbook and AGENTS.md) and wait for approval before touching stop_logic.sh."  # ca0a
